      "url": "https://github.com/kco994553-star/Investment-System1/commit/523e702a806a718d163cfbf62aa3fc29d8c3ef3c",
      "message": "[proposal] FPIA: vendor pure-Python PyYAML 6.0.1 so the FPIA tests run where PyYAML is not installed\n\nFix round 2 (G1) made track_c_fpia_workflows.py read workflow files with PyYAML. The owner full-suite\nworkflows install only pytest and numpy, so on PR CI every FPIA test that reaches the workflow analysis\nwent NOT_RUN or failed with ModuleNotFoundError (60 failed in the offline jobs).\n\n- tools/integration/_vendor/yaml: lib/yaml/*.py of the upstream PyYAML-6.0.1 sdist (sha256 bfdf460b...),\n  byte for byte, with its MIT LICENSE; cyaml.py left out (it imports the compiled yaml._yaml by absolute\n  name). README.md records the provenance and every file digest. No bytecode committed.\n- track_c_fpia_workflows.py imports the vendored copy (package or script mode), never an installed one;\n  loader_info() also records the source and with_libyaml. Detection logic unchanged.\n- track-c-fpia.yml no longer installs PyYAML; the G7 test now asserts that.\n- test_integration_fpia_vendor.py pins the vendored files to the README digests, checks no bytecode or\n  extension modules are tracked, and that both import modes load _vendor/yaml without libyaml.\n- test_integration_fpia_self.py: FPIA's self-placement check now includes the vendored files.\n\nPackaging only: no FPIA check, status or threshold changed. Frozen tools, Track C files and owner paths\nuntouched.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_019znshzTYgyBnuuBmSxdPFN"
    },
